# 12 Momentum-conserving advection for large density ratios {#ch:momentum}

## The problem

Consider water moving to the right at speed $U$ next to air at rest, with the
interface inside cell $i$. During one step, a volume of water $V_w$ crosses
the face into the air cell $i+1$. Physically, momentum $\rho_1 U V_w$
crosses with it. In the BCG scheme of [chapter 11](11_advection), however, the
velocity is advected as if density were uniform, and the face value of $u$ is
reconstructed from cell-centred velocities that mix water and air. The
momentum carried with the water and the mass carried with the water are
computed by different formulas. With $\rho_1/\rho_2 = 816$, a tiny error in
the momentum per unit volume of water becomes a large velocity in a cell
that is mostly air: the air near the crest accelerates, the time step
collapses and the interface breaks up. The remedy, proposed by Rudman
\[[12](references)\] and implemented in `navier-stokes/conserving.h`, is to
move momentum with exactly the same geometric fluxes as the volume fraction.

## Splitting momentum by phase

Define the momentum per unit volume of each phase,
$$
\mathbf{q}_1 = f\rho_1\mathbf{u},\qquad \mathbf{q}_2 = (1 - f)\rho_2\mathbf{u},
\qquad \rho\mathbf{u}= \mathbf{q}_1 + \mathbf{q}_2.
$$
Each component of $\mathbf{q}_1$ is a quantity confined to the water, so it
can be transported as a *VOF tracer* of $f$: its flux through a face is
the volume of water crossing the face times the water momentum per unit
water volume, $\rho_1 u$. Likewise $\mathbf{q}_2$ is a tracer of $1 - f$
(the `inverse` attribute). In each direction-split sweep
([chapter 17](17_vof)), for a face with upwind cell $i$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{volume flux of water:}\quad & F_f = c_f\,u_f, \\
\text{flux of } q_1:\quad & F_{q_1} = \Big[\rho_1 u_i + s\min(1,1 - s\,u_n)\,\partial_x(\rho_1 u)_i\,\frac{\Delta}{2}\Big]\,c_f\,u_f, \\
\text{flux of } q_2:\quad & F_{q_2} = \Big[\rho_2 u_i + \dots\Big]\,(1 - c_f)\,u_f,
\end{aligned}
$$
where $c_f$ is the fraction of the swept volume that is water, computed from
the reconstructed interface ([section 17.3](17_vof#sec:vofflux)). The bracket is the BCG
upwind value of [section 11.2](11_advection#sec:bcg-derivation) without the transverse terms; its
slope is computed only from cells that are mostly of the same phase
(`vof_concentration_gradient_x`), so air velocities never enter the
water momentum flux and vice versa.

