## The update and the recovery of velocity

For each sweep, $f$, $q_1$ and $q_2$ are updated together with the
one-dimensional equations of [section 17.2](17_vof#sec:split):
$$
\begin{aligned}
f_i &\mathrel{+}= \frac{\Delta t}{\Delta}\left[F_{f,{i-\frac 12}} - F_{f,{i+\frac 12}} + \chi_i\,(u_{f,{i+\frac 12}} - u_{f,{i-\frac 12}})\right],\\
q_{1,i} &\mathrel{+}= \frac{\Delta t}{\Delta}\left[F_{q_1,{i-\frac 12}} - F_{q_1,{i+\frac 12}} + \chi_i\,\rho_1 u_i\,(u_{f,{i+\frac 12}} - u_{f,{i-\frac 12}})\right],
\end{aligned}
$$
where $\chi_i = 1$ if $f_i > 0.5$ and $0$ otherwise (the step function of
Weymouth and Yue, [section 17.4](17_vof#sec:wy)). After all sweeps the velocity is
recovered from the total momentum and the new density:
$$
\mathbf{u}^\star = \frac{\mathbf{q}_1 + \mathbf{q}_2}{\rho(f^{n+\frac 12})}.
$$

> **In the code: `navier-stokes/conserving.h`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> q1.x[] = fc*rho1*u.x[];   q2.x[] = (1. - fc)*rho2*u.x[];
> q2.x.inverse = true;
> f.tracers = list_concat (tracers, (scalar *){q1, q2});
> vof_advection ({f}, i);
> u.x[] = (q1.x[] + q2.x[])/rho(f[]);
> ~~~

## Why it works: a uniform flow stays uniform

Suppose $\mathbf{u}= U$ everywhere at the start of the step. Then
$\rho_1 u$ is the constant $\rho_1 U$, its slope is zero, and
$F_{q_1} = \rho_1 U\,F_f$ on every face. The compression terms also match:
$\chi_i\rho_1 u_i = \rho_1 U\chi_i$. So the $q_1$ update is exactly
$\rho_1 U$ times the $f$ update, and $q_1^{\text{new}} = \rho_1 U f^{\text{new}}$.
The same holds for $q_2$ with $1 - f$. Therefore
$$
\mathbf{u}^{\text{new}} = \frac{\rho_1 U f^{\text{new}} + \rho_2 U(1 - f^{\text{new}})}{\rho(f^{\text{new}})} = U
$$
exactly, whatever the density ratio and however the interface moved. The
non-conservative BCG scheme has no such property: moving density with one
scheme and velocity with another lets the two disagree in interface cells.

## Conservation on the adaptive mesh {#sec:momentum-refine}

When cells are refined or coarsened, plain bilinear interpolation of $\mathbf{u}$
would change the total momentum because density differs between children.
`conserving.h` replaces the operators:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{restriction:}\quad & u_{\text{parent}} = \frac{\sum_c c_{m,c}\,\rho(f_c)\,u_c}{2^d\,c_{m,p}\,\rho(f_p)},\\
\text{refinement:}\quad & u_c = u_c^{\text{bilinear}} + \delta u,\quad
 \delta u \text{ chosen so that } \sum_c c_{m,c}\,\rho(f_c)\,u_c = 2^d c_{m,p}\,\rho(f_p)\,u_p.
\end{aligned}
$$
These need the children's $f$ before $u$, so the module moves $f$ to the front
of the field list. They conserve total momentum, not the momentum of each
phase separately.

## What changes in the solver

`conserving.h` sets `stokes = true`, which skips the BCG
`advection_term` and the `dt/2` projection that goes with it.
Because `centered.h` then no longer computes a CFL step, the module
overloads `stability` to call `timestep(uf, dtmax)` itself. The
`vof` event now transports $f$, $q_1$ and $q_2$ together and hides the
interface list from the default `vof` event so $f$ is not moved twice.

